;; The test tool given -g. Its Simplify then keeps the handler of a try that
;; a raise of a kind which records a backtrace reaches, where without -g it
;; makes an ordinary continuation of it. The validation makes ordinary
;; continuations of such handlers in the term before only; so, where it
;; refuses the two terms as they are, the tool asks it again of the term
;; after with those handlers made ordinary continuations too (see
;; ../../tools/flsemantics.ml, "The exception handlers of the term after").
;; The rules of ../ocaml/pairs/ also give the tool -g, for a unit whose
;; first line asks it: three such units have a try (its README.md, "Units
;; that are here for their first line"). `dune promote` updates an
;; X.expected. The rules are written out below.
;;
;; try_catch_all, try_reraise: two units of ../ocaml/, whose handler is
;; reached by a raise and by a reraise. X.expected here is what the tool
;; prints, given -g, of ../ocaml/X.fl: the unit is validated, and the line
;; under the verdict says that the validation was asked of the term after
;; with its handlers made ordinary continuations. ../ocaml/X.expected is
;; what the tool prints of the same text without -g: the same verdict, and
;; no such line. With the validation asked of the two terms as they are and
;; no more, the tool given -g says "not validated" of both.
;;
;; two_raises_unread: a unit that the second asking does not validate
;; either. Its handler does not read the exception, and its body raises one
;; of two predefined exceptions. two_raises_unread.expected is what the tool
;; prints of it given -g, "not validated": with the handler made an
;; ordinary continuation, the term after still gives it the exception, a
;; different symbol at each raise, and the validation, which has removed
;; that parameter from the term before, has no rewrite of the term after
;; that removes it. Nothing of the second asking is printed.
;; two_raises_unread_without_g.expected is what the tool prints of the same
;; text without -g: validated. two_raises_unread.fl is the term that the
;; compiler gives Simplify, as ../ocaml/from_ocaml.sh makes it of
;; two_raises_unread.ml, and a rule checks that it still makes it. The unit
;; is in none of the tables and counts of ../ocaml/README.md.

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  try_catch_all.output
  (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -g %{dep:../ocaml/try_catch_all.fl})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff try_catch_all.expected try_catch_all.output)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  try_reraise.output
  (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -g %{dep:../ocaml/try_reraise.fl})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff try_reraise.expected try_reraise.output)))

(rule
 (targets two_raises_unread.fl.made)
 (deps %{ocamlopt})
 (action
  (run
   sh
   %{dep:../ocaml/from_ocaml.sh}
   %{ocamlopt}
   %{dep:two_raises_unread.ml}
   two_raises_unread.fl.made)))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff two_raises_unread.fl two_raises_unread.fl.made)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  two_raises_unread.output
  (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe -g %{dep:two_raises_unread.fl})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff two_raises_unread.expected two_raises_unread.output)))

(rule
 (with-stdout-to
  two_raises_unread_without_g.output
  (run ../../tools/flsemantics.exe %{dep:two_raises_unread.fl})))

(rule
 (alias runtest)
 (action
  (diff
   two_raises_unread_without_g.expected
   two_raises_unread_without_g.output)))
